{
 "title": "Procedural shaders part 1: every shader node, car paint",
 "summary": "The shader nodes one by one, each on its own subdivided Suzanne (the tutor swaps them on one): Principled, Diffuse (blue), Subsurface Scattering (scale 5, a red light behind), Glossy (roughness 0), Glossy with anisotropy, Refraction (roughness 0.4), Translucent (a blue light behind), Sheen, Emission (yellow, 6), Transparent (colour darkened a little), Glass (green), Toon (diffuse and glossy, size 0.25), and the volumes into the Volume socket: Principled Volume (red), Volume Scatter, Volume Absorption (blue). Then a procedural car paint: dark red, roughness 0, coat 1 (coat roughness 0.1), specular IOR level 2.5 and a red specular tint, a noise through a constant colour ramp as the factor of a Mix Color for blue spots, marked as an asset. Beyond the tutorial: a second colourway (midnight blue with gold flakes) on a torus.",
